<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <TargetFrameworks>net481;net9.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>NekoLib.RuntimeTests.Harness</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>NekoLib.RuntimeTests.Harness</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No PackageReference and no ProjectReference, and that is the point.

    This project is shared plumbing for the Phase E runtime scenarios: the exit
    code contract, the check runner, the artifact layout, the environment
    record, and deterministic schedule generation. It is not a test framework -
    it defines no concepts a scenario must conform to and drives nothing. What
    it removes is five divergent copies of the same encanamento.

    Two rules keep it that way, and breaking either is how it would turn into
    the framework the suite forbids:

      1. It never references a product module. A scenario that needs to name
         its provider or the module under test passes a Type in.
      2. Nothing enters it that does not already have two real consumers, or
         that is not part of an explicitly uniform suite contract - exit codes
         and the artifact format being the clearest examples.

    Like every scenario, it stays outside NekoLib.sln.
  -->

</Project>
